this morning we have a follow-up  investigation from 60 minutes and the  Washington Post on the opioid crisis it  kills nearly 100 people every day 100  people in the first joint investigation  out in October whistleblowers revealed  how a new law weakened the DA's ability  to stop suspicious drug shipments within  the US this new investigation focus on  the biggest or pealed case the DEA ever  pursued against a drug company retired  DEA agent Dave Schiller spoke for the  first time to 60 minutes he says that  the settlement against drug company MCUs  in' was too lenient here's a preview of  bill Whitaker's report this is the best  case we've ever had against a major  distributor in the history of the Drug  Enforcement raishin how do we not go  after the number one organization in the  height of the epidemic when people are  dying everywhere doesn't somebody have  to be held accountable McKesson needs to  be held accountable  holding McKesson accountable meant going  after the fifth largest corporation in  the country headquartered in San  Francisco McKesson has 76,000 employees  and earns almost 200 billion dollars a  year in revenues about the same as Exxon  Mobil since the 1990s McKesson has made  billions from the distribution of  addictive opioids before he retired in  August Schiller had supervised dinh  vestigation Zin drug trafficking and  money laundering cases but he considered  the case against McKesson to be the  single most important investigation of  his lifetime what did they do that was  wrong the issue with McKesson was they  were providing millions and millions and  millions of pills to countless  pharmacies throughout the United States  and they did not maintain any sort of  due diligence this wasn't just happening  in Denver Colorado this was happening in  Los Angeles California it was happening  in Detroit Michigan it was happening in  New York City it was a national problem  and nobody wanted to deal with it we are  now joined by Bill Whitaker in the  Washington Post health reporter that's  Lenny Bernstein back again at the table  your first report guys as you know was  jaw-dropping for a lot of people who  didn't were not aware and saw that what  you revealed for the very first time  now you're back saying about DEA and  McKesson together what are they what is  the accusation this time what is it well  the nation the DEA requires that drug  distributors like McKesson report what  they call suspicious orders and these  are orders that are unusually large or  of unusual frequency and back in 2008  McKesson paid a more than 13 million  dollars in a settlement because the DEA  says that they were sending huge numbers  of suspicious orders around the country  shortly after they signed that  settlement DEA investigators saw  McKesson doing pretty much the same  thing sending thousands and thousands  and thousands of suspicious orders  around the country and they said that  since this was the second time that they  found the company doing it they wanted  to come down hard on them they wanted to  use them as an example to the rest of  the industry they wanted to come down  hard but the attorneys in Washington the  DEA attorneys in Washington they were a  little more lenient and that's what our  story's about  Eleni why were they more lenient where  was the gap there between the DEA and  the lawyers well you know if you watch  cop shows you know the investigators are  always aggressive and the attorneys are  always a little bit more cautious and  the attorneys have to evaluate what can  we prove what can we actually win in  court and there is a very large divide  between those two sides in this case  what we didn't realize when the  negotiated settlement was announced in  January was how big the gap was how  egregious the conduct of McKesson was  and why the investigators thought this  was worth so much larger of a punishment  did the lawyers have a specific problem  was it about motivation from McKesson  you know in other words it could have  just been sloppy behavior not intent was  that what the gap was intent is always  difficult to prove in court that's  always a stumbling block what the  investigators said and you'll you'll see  on Sunday is we had intent they knew  what they were doing in a couple of  cases we could prove it  the lawyers disagree it sounds like some  of the politicians felt that they were  duped by this law including Joe Manchin  from West Virginia a state that has a  huge opioid crisis well that's what we  found we went down to Washington and we  were talking to senators there and they  were saying that what for the law that  we did the first piece about that the  DEA said sort of tied their hands when  it came to going after these these  companies the lawmakers it was only a  three page law many of them said they  didn't know what was in it what was my  cousin's response to your investigation  we tried to get someone from the Kissin  McKesson to talk to us on camera and  they declined but we spoke to the lead  attorney many times through the course  of this and he basically told us that  the company and the DEA lawyers all  agreed that it was fruitless to look  back and go back over alleged  wrongdoings excuse me that what they  wanted to do from now on was look  forward and going forward the company  said that it would no longer behave like  this and the DEA now has an independent  monitor in place to watch the company  make sure that they behaved all right  they would occur in Lenny Bernstein  thanks so much and you can see the full  investigation of the DEA is biggest  opioid case in Sunday's edition of The  Washington Post and then Sunday night on  60 minutes right here on CBS

